Q1. Java is often described as both an interpreted and compiled language because of its unique approach to code execution, involving both compilation and interpretation steps. Here’s a breakdown of why this is the case:

i. Compilation Phase (Java Compiler):

Source Code to Bytecode: When you write Java code and save it in a .java file, it needs to be compiled. The Java compiler (javac) takes the human-readable source code and compiles it into an intermediate form called bytecode. This bytecode is stored in .class files.
Platform-Independent Bytecode: The bytecode is not specific to any single machine or operating system. It’s designed to be platform-independent, meaning the same bytecode can run on any device that has a Java Virtual Machine (JVM).


ii. Interpretation Phase (JVM):
Bytecode to Machine Code: The bytecode generated by the compiler is not executed directly by the hardware. Instead, the Java Virtual Machine (JVM) interprets the bytecode, translating it into machine code that is specific to the host operating system and hardware.

Just-In-Time (JIT) Compilation: Although the JVM primarily interprets bytecode, it also uses a technique called Just-In-Time (JIT) compilation. JIT compiles bytecode into native machine code on the fly (just before it is executed), which can improve performance by reducing the overhead of repeated interpretation.
Summary of the Dual Nature:
Compiler Aspect: Java is a compiled language because the source code is compiled into bytecode by the javac compiler.
Interpreter Aspect: Java is also interpreted because the JVM interprets the bytecode at runtime, converting it into machine code specific to the operating system.


Benefits of this Approach:
Portability: The combination of compilation to bytecode and interpretation by the JVM allows Java programs to be "write once, run anywhere" (WORA). The same Java program can run on different platforms without modification.
Performance: The JIT compiler within the JVM enhances performance by compiling frequently executed bytecode into machine code.

